---
// Method sheet — Common Time (2026-11-08). The six parts of the method-sheet duty (werkgruppe spec §3.5).
import MethodSheet from '@/components/experiments/MethodSheet.astro'
import { FAMILIES, INSTRUMENT, MOTIF_MAX } from '@/lib/common-time/arrangement'
import { clock, readPressings, weekName } from '@/lib/common-time/score'

const REPO = 'https://github.com/frankbueltge/frankbueltge.de'
const link = 'underline decoration-line underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-fg'
const pressings = readPressings()
const first = pressings[0]!
const latest = pressings.at(-1)!
const patch = latest.patch
---

<MethodSheet title="Method sheet — Common Time" workHref="/common-time" workLabel="To the work">
  <section class="sheet-reveal mb-10">
    <h2 class="kicker mb-2">What this is</h2>
    <p class="leading-relaxed">
      A week of Common Ground, played. Each ISO week of the ledger becomes about two minutes of music ({clock(latest.audio.seconds)}) in which every
      note that carries data comes from a committed day file: which topic is the theme, which is each day’s melody, which
      instruments sound it, how many ornaments and how dense the crowd. The claim is narrow and checkable: the arrangement is what
      the published rule makes of the ledger, and a test decides it again from the day files for every pressing. The music adds no
      finding to Common Ground. It makes a week of convergence something you can follow by ear.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-8">
    <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold">1. Sources &amp; licences</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      <li>
        Common Ground’s day files — open data (CC0) in <a class={link} href={`${REPO}/tree/main/src/data/trending`}>src/data/trending</a>:
        the converging topics with the links that show where each surfaced, and the signals of every source. Nothing else is read;
        no other work of the house sounds in this one.
      </li>
      <li>
        The pressings — the audio (AAC) in <a class={link} href={`${REPO}/tree/main/public/common-time`}>public/common-time</a> and the
        scores in <a class={link} href={`${REPO}/tree/main/src/data/common-time`}>src/data/common-time</a>, CC BY 4.0 with the rest of
        the lab’s works. The renderer, <a class={link} href={`${REPO}/blob/main/scripts/common-time/press.py`}>scripts/common-time/press.py</a>,
        is Apache 2.0.
      </li>
      <li>
        No samples, no recordings and no model: every sound is synthesised from numbers, by numpy and scipy, and mastered to
        loudness with pyloudnorm.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-8">
    <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold">2. Cadence</h2>
    <p class="leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      Once a week. A workflow presses every ISO week whose Monday and Sunday are both in the ledger and that has no pressing yet,
      on Monday morning after Sunday’s day file has landed; GitHub starts scheduled runs late, so a pressing can arrive hours after
      that. A pressing is never rewritten. The first pressings, from {weekName(first.week)}, the first full week of the ledger, were
      made at once on the day the work began; {pressings.length} are on file now.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-8">
    <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold">3. Processing</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      <li>
        <strong class="text-fg">Form.</strong> {patch.intro_bars} bars of introduction, {patch.day_bars} bars per day from Monday to Sunday,
        {patch.outro_bars} bars of ending, at {patch.bpm} beats a minute, over the chords {patch.progression.filter((c, i, a) => a.indexOf(c) === i).join(', ')},
        two bars each.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-fg">A name as a melody.</strong> The label of a topic as first recorded in the ledger, accents read as their
        letters, is reduced to its vowels; u, e, i, o and a land on F, G, A, C and D, one eighth note each, the last vowel of a word a
        quarter, at most {MOTIF_MAX} notes. This is the soggetto cavato without the fa, chosen so that no two notes in the piece are a
        semitone apart. A label without a Latin vowel sings a single F.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-fg">Theme.</strong> The topic on the most days of the week; ties go to the one carried by more families in
        all, then to the one seen first. Its melody repeats once a bar, or every two bars when it is longer than one, on every day
        it was on the charts.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-fg">Melody.</strong> On each day, the other topic carried by the most platform families; ties go to the one
        on more days of the week, then to the ledger’s own order. It is played at half speed, twice, by the instrument of every
        family that carried it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-fg">Ornaments and crowd.</strong> Every other converging topic sounds the first note of its melody once, the
        topics spread evenly over the day’s {patch.day_bars * 16} sixteenths in the ledger’s order. The crowd ticks as many times a bar as
        16 × the day’s signals ÷ the most signals any day of the ledger has held, at least once.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-fg">Families.</strong>
        {FAMILIES.map((f, i) => (
          <span>{INSTRUMENT[f]}: {patch.platforms[f].join(', ')}{f === patch.fallback_family && ', and any publisher that is no platform (the news outlets behind Google News)'}{i < FAMILIES.length - 1 ? '; ' : '.'}</span>
        ))}
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-fg">Levels.</strong> Every voice is set to a fixed loudness on an ordinary day of the ledger, the most recent day
        with the median number of converging topics up to the week’s end, played seven times; the week itself never sets a level.
        The finished pressing is brought to −14 LUFS with a true-peak ceiling of −1 dBTP.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-fg">Receipts.</strong> The score of each week names every day file with its sha256, the archive it scaled
        against, the arrangement, the hashes of the rendered sound and the versions that made it. The guard test
        (<a class={link} href={`${REPO}/blob/main/src/lib/common-time/score.test.ts`}>score.test.ts</a>) checks the audio against its hash,
        the day files against theirs, and the arrangement against the rule, decided again from the ledger in TypeScript.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-8">
    <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold">4. Limits of the method</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      <li>Music is no measurement. Nothing heard here is a finding beyond Common Ground’s own; the piece only makes its week audible.</li>
      <li>
        The mapping from vowels to notes is a convention, not a property of the topic: two names with the same vowels sing the same
        melody, and a name in a script without Latin vowels sings nothing of itself.
      </li>
      <li>
        Which platform is which instrument is a choice, made once and printed above. A melody’s fullness counts families, not
        platforms or outlets, so ten outlets behind Google News count as one voice.
      </li>
      <li>
        The crowd moves little: the sources hold about as many signals every day, so the tick is close to constant. It is kept
        because it is what the ledger says, not because it varies.
      </li>
      <li>
        The sound is reproducible where it was made: the same renderer, versions and platform give the same samples, whose hash is
        in the score. A machine with another floating-point library may differ in the last bits; the arrangement does not.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-8">
    <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold">5. Compute footprint</h2>
    <p class="leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      Seconds of CPU per week on the workflow’s runner, no paid service, no GPU. A pressing adds about two megabytes of audio and
      a score of a few dozen kilobytes to the repository, roughly a hundred megabytes a year.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-8">
    <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold">6. Change log</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      <li>
        2026-11-08 — begun, after three listening probes; the third, Common Ground alone, was the one that held. Weeks
        {first.week.slice(-2)} to {latest.week.slice(-2)} pressed at once.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</MethodSheet>
